<!-- Aura-SOC - rule 100665 (level 0). Domain: threat_hunting,linux,mitre_gaps,
     Naming convention and load-order trap: see rules/README.md -->
<group name="threat_hunting,linux,mitre_gaps,">

  <!-- Exclusion for 100653: the Wazuh agent's SCA / rootcheck checks inspect the
       permissions of /etc/shadow (CIS Debian policies) on every scan, in a burst
       of four `stat` calls (/etc/shadow, /etc/shadow-, /etc/gshadow,
       /etc/gshadow-).

       WAS RULE 100642, RENUMBERED 2026-08-01 - and that renumbering is the
       actual fix. An `if_sid` child is only linked if its parent is ALREADY
       loaded, and files load in alphabetical (= numerical) order: 100642 was
       read before 100653 existed, so this exclusion was never attached to
       anything. It had been dead since the day it was written, silently. Any
       exclusion must therefore carry an id GREATER than the rule it excludes -
       verified for the whole ruleset, this was the only offender.

       DISCRIMINANT CORRECTED at the same time (155 false positives in 3 days
       across five hosts). The former version filtered on `audit.exe
       ^/var/ossec/`, which would never have matched either: the scan runs the
       SYSTEM binary, so audit.exe is /usr/bin/stat (or /usr/bin/coreutils on
       the Proxmox sensors). What identifies the scan is the daemon's working
       directory (/var/ossec, wazuh-modulesd's cwd) together with the absence of
       a login session (auid unset - no human behind that execve).

       Residual risk, accepted: a process with no login session that chdir's to
       /var/ossec before running `stat /etc/shadow`. An actual READ of the file
       is not covered by this exclusion - that path is the watch, i.e. rule
       100643. -->

  <rule id="100665" level="0">
    <if_sid>100653</if_sid>
    <field name="audit.cwd" type="pcre2">^/var/ossec$</field>
    <field name="audit.auid" type="pcre2">^4294967295$</field>
    <description>Exclusion 100653: Wazuh agent SCA/rootcheck check</description>
  </rule>

</group>
